---
import Doc from "../layouts/Doc.astro";

const sections = [
  { id: "volatility", title: "Volatility is not constant" },
  { id: "tails", title: "Prices jump" },
  { id: "probability", title: "Chance of profit is not a forecast" },
  { id: "carry", title: "Rates, dividends and costs" },
  { id: "greeks", title: "Greeks are local" },
  { id: "structure", title: "Positions and expiries" },
  { id: "numerics", title: "Numerical error" },
  { id: "market", title: "No market data" },
];
---

<Doc
  title="Where the model is wrong"
  kicker="Options risk tool · limits"
  description="The assumptions behind the options risk tool, where real markets break them, and how much it matters."
  lede="A model is a set of assumptions. Knowing where they fail matters as much as the formulas, so here they are, roughly in order of how much they matter for the numbers on screen."
  sections={sections}
>
  <h2 id="volatility">Volatility is not constant</h2>
  <div class="limit">
    <span class="tag">Large effect</span>
    <h3>One number per leg, fixed for its whole life</h3>
    <p>
      Black-Scholes assumes each option's volatility stays the same until expiry, whatever the price does. Real implied
      volatility moves every day, usually rising when equities fall. It also differs by strike (the smile, or skew) and by
      expiry (the term structure).
    </p>
    <p>
      The tool gives each leg its own volatility, so a position can reflect today's skew. But when the price moves on the
      chart, each leg keeps the volatility it started with (a "sticky strike" view). In a real sell-off, out-of-the-money
      puts usually become dearer than that, so the chart understates the value of long puts and the losses on short ones.
    </p>
    <p class="fix">
      Partial remedy in the tool: the volatility slider multiplies every leg's volatility by the same factor, and the
      scenario grid adds the same number of points to every leg. Neither can steepen the skew or twist the term
      structure.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="tails">Prices jump</h2>
  <div class="limit">
    <span class="tag">Large effect</span>
    <h3>Log-returns are assumed normal; real ones have fat tails</h3>
    <p>
      The model's price moves are continuous and lognormal. Real prices gap overnight, jump on earnings and crash more
      often than a normal distribution allows. Large moves are several times more common than the model says, so it
      understates the risk of short options far from the money and of any position that loses in a gap.
    </p>
    <p class="fix">
      Earnings are the clearest case: implied volatility is high before the announcement and collapses after it. Splitting
      that out is planned as the IV-crush lab.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="probability">Chance of profit is not a forecast</h2>
  <div class="limit">
    <span class="tag">Often misread</span>
    <h3>It is the market's risk-neutral probability</h3>
    <p>
      The headline chance of profit assumes the stock grows at the interest rate. That is the distribution under which
      option prices are fair, not a prediction. Real stocks are expected to earn a risk premium, and the tool lets you enter
      your own expected return to see the difference.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two more simplifications: a position with legs at different volatilities is described by one weighted volatility,
      and the probability ignores the fat tails above. For a short strangle, the chance of profit can look high while the
      expected loss in the tail is large. Read it together with the scenario grid, never alone.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="carry">Rates, dividends and costs</h2>
  <div class="limit">
    <span class="tag mild">Medium effect</span>
    <h3>Continuous dividends, one flat rate, no frictions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Dividends</strong> are a continuous yield. Real dividends are discrete cash payments on known dates, and the
        day before an ex-dividend date is exactly when an American call is most often worth exercising. The tree with a
        smooth yield misses that.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Interest</strong> is one flat, continuously compounded rate for every expiry, with no difference between
        borrowing and lending, and no cost to borrow stock for short sales.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Trading costs</strong>: no bid-ask spread in the payoff and risk views, no commissions, no margin, and no
        financing of the premium paid. Only the arbitrage checker uses bids and asks.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Contract size</strong> is fixed at 100 shares; adjusted contracts after splits or mergers are not handled.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="greeks">Greeks are local</h2>
  <div class="limit">
    <span class="tag mild">Medium effect</span>
    <h3>They describe small moves, measured now</h3>
    <p>
      Delta, gamma, theta and vega are slopes at today's price, date and volatility. They change as any of those change,
      which is why the P/L explain shows an "unexplained" part. That part grows quickly with the size of the move and near
      expiry, where gamma is concentrated at the strike. For large moves, trust the scenario grid, which reprices fully,
      over the Greeks.
    </p>
    <p>
      American Greeks are measured by nudging the inputs and repricing a tree, so they carry a little numerical noise,
      especially close to the early-exercise boundary.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="structure">Positions and expiries</h2>
  <div class="limit">
    <span class="tag mild">Medium effect</span>
    <h3>The model stops at the first expiry</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Everything is valued up to the first expiry in the position. For a calendar spread, the "at expiry" curve values
        the longer leg with Black-Scholes at its remaining time and its starting volatility, which is exactly where the
        volatility assumption above bites hardest.
      </li>
      <li>
        Early assignment is not modelled. A short American option is valued at its tree price, which includes the early
        exercise premium, but the tool never assigns it, so the leg never disappears from the position early as it can in
        practice.
      </li>
      <li>
        Pin risk, settlement details and the choice between exercising and selling an option at expiry are ignored. An
        option at expiry is worth its intrinsic value, no more.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="numerics">Numerical error</h2>
  <div class="limit">
    <span class="tag mild">Small effect</span>
    <h3>Small and measured</h3>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>Size</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Reading between profit-grid points (European)</td><td>Under 1% of the money at stake, checked across six strategies</td></tr>
        <tr><td>American grids (31-step tree)</td><td>About 2 cents per share against a 1,601-step tree</td></tr>
        <tr><td>American single prices (201-step Leisen-Reimer)</td><td>Well under a cent per share</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Black-Scholes prices and Greeks</td><td>Within 0.000001 of QuantLib</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Chance of profit</td><td>Exact given the model: a difference of normal probabilities at root-found break-evens</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Model error, the sections above, is far larger than any of these.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="market">No market data</h2>
  <div class="limit">
    <span class="tag mild">By design</span>
    <h3>Everything is typed in</h3>
    <p>
      There are no live or delayed quotes. The volatility you enter is an input, not a market observation, and the option
      chain is a model chain drawn from three smile parameters, not a real one. The tool teaches how a position behaves
      under stated assumptions. It does not tell you what an option is worth in the market today, and nothing here is
      investment advice.
    </p>
  </div>
</Doc>
